  "modules": [
    {
      "title": "Module 1 \u00b7 Simultaneous Linear Equations",
      "html": "<p><b>Elimination:</b> add/subtract multiples of the equations to remove one variable. <b>Substitution:</b> solve one equation for a variable and substitute. <b>Graphical:</b> intersect the two lines.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Using elimination or substitution gives $x=8$, $y=2$.</li><li>Using elimination or substitution gives $x=3$, $y=8$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+3y=14$ and $5x+2y=44$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=8,\\ y=2</p><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+1y=11$ and $2x+1y=14$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=3,\\ y=8</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Solve $x + y = 7$, $x - y = 3$.",
          "a": "Add: $2x = 10 \\Rightarrow x = 5$. Then $y = 7 - 5 = 2$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 2 \u00b7 Linear & Quadratic Simultaneously",
      "html": "<p>Make one variable the subject in the linear equation, substitute into the quadratic, solve the resulting quadratic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Using elimination or substitution gives $x=8$, $y=2$.</li><li>Using elimination or substitution gives $x=3$, $y=8$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+3y=14$ and $5x+2y=44$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=8,\\ y=2</p><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+1y=11$ and $2x+1y=14$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=3,\\ y=8</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Solve simultaneously: $1x+3y=14$ and $5x+2y=44$. \u2014 x=8,\\ y=2.</li><li>Solve simultaneously: $1x+1y=11$ and $2x+1y=14$. \u2014 x=3,\\ y=8.</li><li>Solve simultaneously: $4x+1y=44$ and $1x+5y=49$. \u2014 x=9,\\ y=8.</li><li>Solve simultaneously: $4x+1y=26$ and $5x+3y=36$. \u2014 x=6,\\ y=2.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Solve $y = x+1$ and $y = x^2 - 1$.",
          "a": "$x^2 - 1 = x + 1 \\Rightarrow x^2 - x - 2 = 0 \\Rightarrow (x-2)(x+1)=0$. So $x=2, y=3$ or $x=-1, y=0$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 3 \u00b7 Completing the Square & the Formula",
      "html": "<p>For $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ with $a\\ne 0$, the roots are given by:</p>$$x = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}$$<p>Equivalently, by completing the square:</p>$$a\\left(x+\\dfrac{b}{2a}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{4a}$$<h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Using elimination or substitution gives $x=8$, $y=2$.</li><li>Using elimination or substitution gives $x=3$, $y=8$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+3y=14$ and $5x+2y=44$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=8,\\ y=2</p><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+1y=11$ and $2x+1y=14$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=3,\\ y=8</p><h4>More key points</h4><ul><li>Quadratic formula is: \u2014 $$ -b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4ac} / 2a $$.</li><li>Quadratic formula term $$ b^2 - 4ac $$ is: \u2014 Discriminant.</li><li>Perimeter of a square with side 5cm. \u2014 20cm.</li><li>Length of an arc formula: \u2014 $$ \\frac{\\theta}{360} \\times 2\\pi r $$.</li></ul>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Solve $x^2 + 4x - 5 = 0$ by the formula.",
          "a": "$a=1, b=4, c=-5$. $x = \\dfrac{-4\\pm\\sqrt{16+20}}{2} = \\dfrac{-4\\pm 6}{2}$. So $x=1$ or $x=-5$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Module 4 \u00b7 Linear Inequalities",
      "html": "<p>Treat like equations, but <b>reverse the inequality</b> when multiplying/dividing by a negative. For two variables, sketch the boundary line (dashed if strict, solid if inclusive), then shade the region satisfying the inequality.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>Using elimination or substitution gives $x=8$, $y=2$.</li><li>Using elimination or substitution gives $x=3$, $y=8$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+3y=14$ and $5x+2y=44$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=8,\\ y=2</p><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+1y=11$ and $2x+1y=14$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=3,\\ y=8</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "Solve $-3x + 5 < 14$.",
          "a": "$-3x < 9 \\Rightarrow x > -3$ (sign flipped)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revision and exam focus",
      "html": "<p>Revise the whole topic by working through its key points in order, then test yourself with the 50-question quiz for this topic. Pay attention to definitions, differences between similar terms, and questions that ask you to apply the topic rather than only recall it.</p><h4>Worked examples</h4><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+3y=14$ and $5x+2y=44$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=8,\\ y=2 \u2014 Using elimination or substitution gives $x=8$, $y=2$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $1x+1y=11$ and $2x+1y=14$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=3,\\ y=8 \u2014 Using elimination or substitution gives $x=3$, $y=8$.</p><p><b>Q:</b> Solve simultaneously: $4x+1y=44$ and $1x+5y=49$.<br/><b>A:</b> x=9,\\ y=8 \u2014 Using elimination or substitution gives $x=9$, $y=8$.</p>",
      "examples": [
